From d6d4664f7d1998aa4f5fd61043b4f438104767a4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: paulkoan <paulkoan@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 19:51:24 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Linux: the four MSVC-only fixes, the full PTX list, and a
 `serve` mode that speaks our worker protocol

gcc 14 / glslang 16.6.0 build the whole thing: 15/15 shaders to SPIR-V, all 78 PTX
kernels, a dlss5vk that runs the network on the GPU box (9.960 ms median at 2560x1440
on a 4080 SUPER, 249 dispatches, PTX route).

Four fixes, all of them things MSVC accepted:

  * src/kernels.h: `= Chain()` as a default argument constructs a nested type with
    default member initializers while that type is still incomplete. Declared a
    static default-constructed Chain instead; same thing to every caller.
  * src/vk_context.h, src/nr_model.h: memcpy without <cstring>.
  * scripts/build_linux.sh: VK_ENABLE_BETA_EXTENSIONS must reach volk.c, or volk.h
    never declares the VK_NV_cuda_kernel_launch entry points (five undefined
    references at link). The PowerShell build gets the define for free by compiling
    volk.c in the same cl invocation.

And the bug that replaced one of them: the script's PTX list is a hand copy of the
emitter invocations in scripts/build_shaders.ps1, and it was copied from an incomplete
read of that file - the last two loops were missing, so 12 of 78 kernels were never
generated: the five fused 32-channel block variants (block32_e4m3_f2/66/74/130/48) and
the seven split-512 GEMMs (gemmv_e4m3_K{512,256,4096,1024}_f*_s1_m96). Nothing failed at
build time - the host resolves kernel files by name when a dispatch is first reached -
so it surfaced as

    error: cannot read PTX kernel ./build/shaders/../ptx/block32_e4m3_f74.ptx

The script now emits all 78 and checks the count, because the failure mode of a short
list is a run-time error that names the missing file and not the reason.

New: `dlss5vk serve`, so the pipeline can run the pass without Wine.

It speaks the wire format native/dlss5-feed-host64.cpp speaks, byte for byte, so
pointing NS_WORKER_CMD at it is the whole integration - the startup header, FRAME_MAGIC
with colour and motion inline, OUT_MAGIC with the pixels back. No upscaling (the header's
full size must be 0 or the work size), no shared-memory channel (SHMI is refused with
ok=0, the documented fallback).

The temporal loop, both halves. serve expands the frame it published last into a proxy of
its own and hands it to preprocess.comp, which samples it for lanes 7-9 where the demo's
nr_preprocess.comp samples its own history surface; composite_serve.comp then blends it
in, which is docs/frame.md's composition:

    weight = clamp(sigmoid(head.a) * blend_scale, 0, 1)          # the head's blend logit
    neural = lerp(proxy + head.rgb / 4, history, weight)

with the demo's own expression for the weight - 1 / (1 + exp2(-x * 1.442695)), constant
included, because the two have to agree bit for bit - and blend_scale read out of the
model (block70.layer0.blend_scale, printed at startup rather than written into the
source: a hard-coded weight would quietly disagree with the next model file).

The history half is the whole of it now: the composite reprojects and filters the history
rather than reading the pixel. The client's motion field is bound to the composite as it
arrives on the wire (binding 2, 2 x f16 per pixel: current -> previous, work-resolution
pixels, y down - guides.py's units, "NGX consumes current-to-previous motion in pixel
units") and the shader reads it the way the demo's nr_common.glsl reads motionTex, sampling
five taps with the bilinear-weight trick - the four axis taps and the centre, clamped to
the valid rectangle, combined in the demo's order and normalized by the weight sum. The
history is a buffer and not a sampled image, so historyFetch spells out the bilinear a
texture unit performs for the demo's textureLod; the tap positions, the weights and the
combination are the demo's expression for expression, and check_composite.py transliterates
both sides over random motion and a random history field rather than taking that on trust.

Staying in pixels instead of the demo's uv round trip is deliberate: with a zero field the
position is the pixel's own centre, where the weights collapse to the centre tap and the
published byte is the one the blend was measured at (r2026-10-12-d) - a property a run on
the box can check, and one no uv round trip would hold, since (x / valid) * valid is not x.

The motion field is created once, zeroed, and uploaded only when the caller sends one, so
a caller that declares no field (--no-motion, which exists because the field is 14.7MB at
1440p) pays nothing and still exercises the filter's zero-motion path.

What the field does not carry is the demo's per-pixel "is there a history at that previous
position" flag: the demo's motionTex has it as a third component, because zero motion means
the history is at this same pixel and so cannot also mean there is none. The wire's field
has no room for it. The one case readable from the position is a reprojection that leaves
the valid rectangle - those pixels have no history and skip the blend, which is the demo's
treatment of a pixel whose previous position was off screen. Disocclusion stays the
network's blend logit to reject, as the demo says it always was.

A reset frame, and a frame served in bypass, have no history at all: the blend is skipped
and the residual is published alone - which is why the parity gate (src/main.cpp passes no
history) cannot see any of this.

One extra dispatch for the history expand and no host bytes moved is the loop's whole
cost. The truncation to the half grid is now a single publication point after the blend,
`history' = truncate_to_half(neural)` as docs/frame.md has it; with no history that is
the same expression main.cpp's `composed()` truncates, so no published byte moves.

With FRAME_FLAG_BYPASS the pixels come back untouched and no device is created, which is
what lets the framing, the header parse, the refusals and the end-of-stream handshake be
tested on a machine with no GPU.

Also lands: `--model`/`--shaders`/`--frames` for the mode, the warm record that pays the
249-kernel JIT burst on a zero proxy where the log can say what the wait was, and a
per-frame timing line on stderr.
