+// dlss5vk serve: the NR pass as a worker process, on this side of Wine.
+//
+//   dlss5vk serve --model <nr model dir> [--shaders <dir>] [--frames N]
+//
+// It speaks the wire format native/dlss5-feed-host64.cpp speaks - the exact bytes
+// protocol.py packs and unpacks - so the pipeline can point NS_WORKER_CMD at this
+// binary instead of `wine nvngx.dll --live` and nothing else has to change:
+//
+//   startup  VIDEO_MAGIC  "<10I4f2I"  64 bytes  work size + the NR parameters
+//   frame    FRAME_MAGIC  "<4Iq"      24 bytes  then colour RGBA8 + motion f16, inline
+//   reply    OUT_MAGIC    "<5Iq"      28 bytes  then colour RGBA8 (work_w * work_h * 4)
+//
+// What this is not, and says so rather than misbehaving:
+//
+//   * No NGX upscaling. fullWidth/fullHeight must be 0 or the work size. The MVP sends
+//     0 ("colour and work are the same size"); a --work-scale run is rejected here.
+//   * No shared-memory channel. SHMI is answered ok=0, which is the documented
+//     fallback: the client stays on sending frames down the pipe. (Worker.start passes
+//     no shm at all, so the MVP never asks; this is for everything else that might.)
+//   * A history, reprojected and filtered. serve keeps the frame it published last, feeds it
+//     back as lanes 7-9 (the network's temporal input) and blends it in the composite:
+//
+//       weight = clamp(sigmoid(head.a) * block70.layer0.blend_scale, 0, 1)
+//       neural = lerp(proxy + head.rgb / 4, history, weight)      # docs/frame.md
+//
+//     The composite samples that history where the client's motion field says this pixel was -
+//     current -> previous, work-resolution pixels, the units guides.py sends NGX - with the
+//     demo's five-tap Catmull-Rom reconstruction (shaders/composite_serve.comp, and
+//     experiments/opendlss_port/check_composite.py, which transliterates both sides). The field
+//     is the wire's; nothing is derived from the picture. A zero field ("The MVP has no real
+//     motion vectors", minimal/loop.py) puts the previous position at this pixel's own centre,
+//     where the five-tap is the centre tap and nothing else - so the fixture's bytes are what
+//     they were before the filter existed, which is what a run on the box can check. The blend
+//     weight is the network's own, per pixel; blend_scale is read from the model rather than
+//     assumed. What is NOT here: the demo's style / tone-upgrade / display path, which is this
+//     pipeline's display stage and not NR.
+//
+//     What the field does not carry is the demo's per-pixel "is there a history" flag
+//     (motionTex.z): a reprojection that leaves the valid rectangle is the one case readable
+//     from the position, and those pixels skip the blend. Disocclusion is not visible to
+//     either and stays the network's blend logit to reject.
+//
+// The proxy is the incoming frame's code values, f16-rounded. The network sees a
+// display proxy (docs/frame.md) and an SDR video frame is already display-referred, so
+// there is no tone map to apply on this side. A stream is 8-bit, which is the precision
+// floor of this path and the first thing to suspect if the result looks banded.
